<title>USA: UN SAYS OIL OVERSEERS APPROVE THIRD IRAQ CONTRACT.</title>
<headline>UN SAYS OIL OVERSEERS APPROVE THIRD IRAQ CONTRACT.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.N. overseers approved a third Iraqi oil contract late Monday for a total of 2.7 million barrels, U.N. spokesman Hiro Ueki said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>&quot;A third contract has been approved for 2.7 million barrels,&quot; said Ueki.</p>
<p>No further details were immediately available, but an informed U.N. source said the third contract went to an Italian oil company.</p>
<p>Monday, the U.N. announced two contract approvals for a total of 11.62 million barrels, with one Russian and one French company taking the oil.</p>
<p>The latest contract approval brings the barrel total for second round, June-December, &quot;oil-for-food&quot; contracts to 14.32 million barrels.</p>
<p>Iraq is allowed to sell $2.14 billion worth of crude from June 8 to Dec 6, in two separate 90-day periods. The first period expires Sept 5.</p>
<p>More than two-thirds of the oil receipts are earmarked for purchases of food and medicine for civilians suffering from sanctions imposed after Iraq invaded Kuwait in August, 1990.</p>
<p>((Patrick Connole, NY Oil Desk, +1 212 859 1828))</p>
